{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the year of the death.</b> The earlier ones said that the word מות is like צרעה, because at the time he entered the sanctuary to offer incense there was an earthquake; this too is valid. And it may be according to its plain meaning, that in the year he prophesied, Uzziah died. And there is no objection concerning 'in the days of Uzziah' (Isaiah 1:1), since [that span] was months, and this is the beginning of Isaiah's prophecy." "<b>High and exalted.</b> It is a description of the throne, not as many said. And the meaning is that it is above the living creatures, as I shall explain in the book of Ezekiel." "<b>And its skirts.</b> They are the skirts of the throne, for it is the custom of kings to have garments upon their thrones." "<b>Seraphim.</b> He called them seraphim because they burned his mouth." "And the meaning of 'standing above him' is like 'standing by him, on his right and on his left' (1 Kings 22:19). And the Torah speaks in the language of human beings so that human beings may understand, for thus is the way of great kings." "And the meaning of these wings I shall explain in the Visions of Ezekiel." "And the covering of the face is like 'and Moses hid his face' (Exodus 3:6), and likewise his feet, on account of the honor." "<b>And with two he flies.</b> To fulfill the mission of HaShem."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The word מות is glossed as צרעה, literally 'wasp'; the reference appears to be to an earthquake (רעש) at the time of Uzziah's intrusion into the sanctuary (2 Chronicles 26:16–19), though the exact point of the comparison is compressed."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:30.988878742Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}